    "q-156657": {
      "dom_id": "step-q156657",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $5z\\le -45$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $5.$ To isolate $z$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $5.$ So, we divide both sides of the equation by $5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z & \\le -45 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\le \\frac{-45}{5} \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\le -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $5$ from the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z}{5} & \\le -9 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}z}{\\cancel{5}} & \\le -9 \\\\ z & \\le -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\le -9.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/bd4ccae5ea797d6c41e83c426b76491316552883e831bb82d82345d91773e4a3.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
