    "q-155556": {
      "dom_id": "step-q155556",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $-\\frac{a}{4}>-2$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $a$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $a$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{a}{4}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}a}{\\cancel{4}} & <8 \\\\ a & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<8.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
