    "q-44882": {
      "dom_id": "step-q44882",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $-3x+2\\ge 5$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $2$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & \\ge 5 \\\\ -3x+2-2 & \\ge 5-2 \\\\ -3x+0 & \\ge 3 \\\\ -3x & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3}\\, & \\le \\,\\,\\frac{3}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-3}x}{\\cancel{-3}}\\, & \\le \\,\\,-1 \\\\ x\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/34610901823137e2c15d3bf4515a7c700469f97cd876961b6ea8d9afadd1d14f.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
