    "q-155561": {
      "dom_id": "step-q155561",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $-5x-7<-2$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $7$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x-7 & <-2 \\\\ -5x-7+7 & <-2+7 \\\\ -5x+0 & <5 \\\\ -5x & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing by $-5.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x\\,\\, & <\\,\\,5 \\\\ \\frac{-5x}{-5}\\, & >\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}x}{\\cancel{-5}}\\, & >\\,\\,-1 \\\\ x\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x>-1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/236731caee3bdbb7fee8922577a4945e97d81ee2876ca5b050c12654b2277a2a.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
